uber is the godfather of ride hailing  they have 5 million drivers worldwide  and operate in 900 different cities to  put that in perspective even walmart  only has 2.3 million employees now of  course uber's employees aren't full-time  like walmart's but together uber drivers  provide ride sharing and delivery  services to 65 million people in america  alone but despite this insanely massive  network uber has never been that  profitable of a company in fact since  they went public they haven't even had a  single 12-month period in which they  were profitable for a little bit it  seemed like uber was finally turning  things around as the company posted  their first profitable quarters last  year but in 2022 things have gotten  worse than ever before as uber just  posted the worst quarterly loss ever  coming in at nearly 6 billion  as you would guess this hasn't been  great news for the stock as uber has  sold off 66 from its all-time high  coincidentally this correlates to a  market cap loss of roughly 66 billion  dollars  and while that is quite devastating it  also makes sense considering uber's  financials for uber to really make  progress forward it they have to first  become profitable so here's why uber has  been struggling to make money and when  the company might finally become  profitable  one of the biggest fundamental issues  with uber's business model is that  they're constantly fighting with  themselves to grow larger here's the  thing for uber's network to be as strong  as possible they need both the drivers  and riders to be on their side but the  issue with this is that the goals of  riders and drivers are exactly opposite  of each other riders want the service to  be as affordable as possible while  drivers want the service to be as  worthwhile as possible so uber is stuck  in this super weird catch-22 situation  if they raise pay for drivers they'll  grow the size of their network but this  is pointless because less people will  find their services appealing on the  other hand if they lower fares they'll  appeal to more customers but this is  once again pointless because there won't  be enough drivers to meet demand clearly  uber isn't quite a pickle but this isn't  that surprising when you take a look at  the history of the taxi industry  the first gas-powered taxis appeared in  new york in 1907 but it was until the  launch of the model t that this industry  really took off soon enough there were  dozens of new taxi companies fighting  for market share and there's really not  much innovation you can do with the taxi  business really there's only one way to  capture more market share and that's by  lowering fares and that's exactly what  all these companies did this trend was  just further accelerated by the great  depression as many of the people who  lost their jobs turned to becoming taxi  drivers all this competition it  completely eliminated the profitability  of the industry and we saw protests  break out across the country these  protests demanded that the government  regulate the tax industry by limiting  the number of taxis in each city and  that's exactly what the government would  do  so becoming a traditional taxi driver  isn't as easy as simply getting a car  and marketing your services you actually  have to get a taxi medallion in most  urban cities across the us  while this did make the tax industry  rather bureaucratic it also affects the  profitability problem the takeaway from  this story is that the tax industry has  never been profitable without  regulations and limitations and given  that neither uber nor any of their  competition abide by any of these  regulations thanks to a few loopholes  it's not surprising that they've been  struggling to make a profit to make  things even worse ride-hailing companies  are extremely well-funded lyft for  example currently has 4.7 billion  dollars in total assets and uber has 32  billion in total assets even with the  recent six billion loss  so for these guys it's not just a race  to the bottom it's a race to see who  will go bankrupt or get bought out first  and the survivor takes it all the bright  side for uber is that if they do make it  to the other side they'll have such  massive economies of skill that they'll  basically become untouchable but in the  meantime they're burning money as always  likely the only technology that can  disrupt uber's dominance in such a  future is robo taxis machines don't care  about getting paid a decent amount and  they rarely have to rest or take a break  so this is clearly what uber needs to  get to the next level but it can also be  used against them uber is of course well  aware of this threat and that's why  they're investing so heavily in robo  taxis but this hasn't exactly worked out  for them in fact in late 2020 uber  ditched the idea of creating their own  robo taxis by selling their robotics  division to a company called aurora now  uber did take on a stake in aurora so  it's not like they completely gave up on  robo taxis but they did limit exposure  and give up on the idea of developing an  in-house robo taxi and while this might  sound like a questionable decision at  first glance it was really the only  choice they had because they don't  really have a chance against tesla for a  couple of reasons starting with data  collection data is likely the single  most important factor in developing  machine learning and artificial  intelligence especially when it comes to  edge cases and with how many cars tesla  already has on the road collecting data  it's basically impossible to catch up to  them in terms of data  way back in april of 2020 tesla already  had a 3.3 billion autopilot miles so  they're probably closing in on 10  billion miles by now  this amount of data is simply not  something that uber can compete against  with a small fleet of test vehicles i  mean even google has only been able to  clock in 20 million miles so the odds  were never that great for uber but even  more importantly than the data lead  tesla has something that no one else has  which is that consumers are literally  paying tesla to develop autonomous  vehicles with companies like uber and  google they have to make money from  their main businesses or raise money to  invest in robo taxis but tesla already  has hundreds of thousands of customers  who are paying them at 12 000 per car to  continue developing autonomy this is  clearly not a battle that even google  much less uber can win and uber did  eventually realize this but not before  sinking 2.5 billion to europa taxis  themselves and while it makes sense why  they made this bet this bet has  unfortunately been weighing down on  uber's bottom line for years  fighting in a race to the bottom and  being forced to ditch robo taxis are  already bad enough but these issues were  just made even worse by leadership  usually when we talk about bad  leadership we're generally talking about  nba businessmen who prioritize  short-term profits over long-term  innovation  but when it comes to uber we're actually  talking about the co-founder travis  kalanick travis wasn't exactly a bad  leader from a business perspective per  se  he was expanding the company across the  globe and he was heavily investing in  self-driving which seemed like the right  move at the time the real problem with  travis however was all the drama that he  brought with him first of all travis was  infamous for overseeing a rather toxic  workplace culture  allegedly he had a bad habit of turning  a blind eye to gender-based  discrimination and harassment and in  february of 2017 a former uber employee  would publicly blast the company for  these practices this prompted a full-on  investigation into the company's  workplace culture by former u.s attorney  general eric holder eric's investigation  would result in a slur of  recommendations for uber one of which  was reducing travis's role at the  company this was already a pretty bad  situation for travis but things would  just get even worse when travis himself  would get accused of harassment as this  entire harassment case was unfolding  travis was also being blasted for a  leaked argument with an uber driver in  this argument the uber driver would  question travis about recent changes to  uber black which seemed unfair to  apparently uber had reduced uber black  fears substantially which made it  unprofitable for the driver to continue  driving around with a 97 000 car  at first the two would address the  problem diplomatically but it didn't  take long for things to get out of hand  how much is the mile now 275 you know  what what some people  don't like to take  responsibility for that  they blame everything  good luck good luck to you too but i  know you don't gonna go far  travis's comment that the uber driver  wasn't taking responsibility for his own  actions attracted a lot of criticism  people were quick to call out how travis  had bypassed loads of taxi regulations  and labor lost through uber and how he  was not taking responsibility for any of  that now honestly i think some of the  blame does lie on the uber driver as  well given that buying a 97 000 car  based on uber income is not exactly the  smartest of choices but that's besides  the point travis's allegations and the  comments had destroyed his reputation  and this was enough to kick off the  delete uber movement travis would  eventually be ousted by the board and he  would leave the company altogether and  sell all of his talk in december of 2019  but the damage had already been done  despite all these challenges it does  look like uber is finally turning a  corner they've already expanded all  across the globe so they no longer have  to fight so hard for market share they  have also expanded into adjacent  businesses like food delivery which has  diversified their income on top of this  they've cut losses on self-driving and  travis is no longer involved in the  company whatsoever so it really is just  a matter of leaning up the business and  achieving profitability something else  to note is the vast majority of the  losses last quarter were due to poorly  performing assets as opposed to terrible  cash flow if we take a look at their  ebitda we'll see that uber only lost 250  million last quarter due to the business  itself something that is a bit  concerning is their ebitda has actually  never been positive but fortunately  their revenue and gross profits are at  all-time highs so uber may just need a  couple more quarters to achieve  consistent profitability in fact many  analysts are speculating that 2022 will  be uber's final negative year and that  they'll be consistently breaking even by  the end of 2023  now of course it'll take a couple of  years from there to really build up  profitability but it does seem like uber  can pull in respectable profits by the  end of this decade however this does not  mean that uber is completely out of the  woods because they still have to go up  against tesla it's no secret that tesla  wants to get into the right healing  business with self-driving cars and  though elon's timelines are almost  always too ambitious it seems like  whether it happens in 2025 or 2030 or  2040 tesla will likely be the first one  to achieve fully autonomous cars and  once tesla starts releasing these cars  onto the road things will get quite  challenging for uber either they can buy  teslas themselves or they can continue  keeping up their partnerships with  autonomous companies like aurora but  neither of these are exactly optimal  tesla will always be able to offer  cheaper ride healing services than uber  buying teslas and then putting them on  the road conversely it's also not clear  how well uber self-driving partnerships  will pan out compared to tesla's  offerings so the long-term future of  uber is still rather uncertain but the  next three to five years should  hopefully be smooth as uber finally  achieves profitability but that's just  what i think do you guys ride ubers  comment that down below also drop a like  if you would like to eat the  responsibility of growing this channel  and of course consider checking out our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing and  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one  you
 

